    title: "US judge rules in favour of foreign students and journalists after Trump limits visas",
    summary: "US judge rules in favour of foreign students and journalists after Trump limits visas Submitted by Yasmine El-Sabawi on Wed, 11/18/2026 - 17:14 The Department of Homeland Security simply could not prove there was widespread fraud or security issues, the judge said US Secretary of State Marco Rubio takes questions from journalists in the Department of State briefing room during his end-of-year press conference, on 19 December 2025 (Mandel Ngan/AFP) Off A US federal judge has thrown out the Trump administration's attempt to limit visas for international students and members of the foreign press after university and media unions filed a lawsuit against the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) in August. Massachusetts District Judge Dennis Saylor said on Monday that the government has been unable to prove there has been widespread visa fraud or national security concerns with this particular visa programme. DHS's move to impose restrictions \"is arbitrary and capricious\", Saylor said in his decision. \"The Final Rule, entitled Establishing a Fixed Time Period of Admission and an Extension of Stay Procedure for Nonimmigrant Academic Students, Exchange Visitors, and Representatives of Foreign Information Media... is hereby VACATED,\" he wrote. Saylor added that DHS is \"seeking to prevent or discourage the very activity - providing educational opportunities for foreign students in the United States - that Congress sought to permit and encourage\". The ruling preserves the \"duration of status\" or \"D/S\" stamp that international students receive on their visas and, by extension, on the visas foreign journalists receive as well. It means they are legally permitted to stay in the country as long as they remain in the status they obtained, either as active students or working journalists, whether it's mere months or several years. US visas are always up to the discretion of the issuing officer. International student and foreign journalist visas - the F, J, and I - are considered \"nonimmigrant\" categories, meaning they do not lead to any kind of permanent residency or citizenship status, unless a person's circumstances change and they qualify to apply for another category. \"International students add to the vibrancy of our campuses, drive innovative research, and support the Commonwealth’s economic vitality,\" Robert J McCarron, president and CEO of the Association of Independent Colleges & Universities in Massachusetts, said in a press release published on Monday, following the judge's ruling. The Trump administration slammed the judge's decision. \"Come on a student visa, take one class per semester, and stay here for decades. According to Judge Dennis Saylor, ICE must permit this rampant abuse of our immigration system,\" James Percival, DHS General Counsel, wrote on X in response to the injunction issued back in September. 'Exceptional relief' DHS first proposed strict time limits on the F, J, and I visas a little over a year ago, and opened up a public comment period, as it is obligated to do before changes take effect. It received 22,000 comments overwhelmingly against the changes. But in July, DHS decided to impose the new restrictions anyway, with a 60-day window before they took effect. Students would have been restricted to four years, and journalists to eight months. In August, NAFSA: Association of International Educators; the Presidents’ Alliance on Higher Education and Immigration; the Association of Independent Colleges and Universities in Massachusetts; the American Federation of Teachers; Graduate Labor Organization AFT Local 6516; International Union United Automobile; Aerospace and Agricultural Implement Workers of America; United Auto Workers Local 2322; and The NewsGuild-CWA all filed a legal complaint demanding an injunction. Will the new Democrats in Congress remake the party of genocide and war? tions in the US, including Pen America; the Society of Professional Journalists; the Online News Association; and the Foreign Press Association, also signed a letter urging members of Congress to block the rule by withholding DHS funds if necessary. \"Journalists already condemn Russia, China, Hong Kong and other countries for abusing the visa process to punish journalists in retaliation for their reporting. This rule puts the United States in the league of repressive and anti-free press governments, and enacting it could lead other countries to impose similar limitations on American journalists working overseas,\" the letter, viewed by Middle East Eye, reads. Some seven hours before the rule went into effect, on 14 September, Saylor issued a temporary injunction. This week's decision ends the case in its current form, though the administration may appeal. The Committee to Protect Journalists told MEE on Wednesday that Saylor's decision is an \"exceptional relief\". \"[It] is really a resounding affirmation of the collective defense of the press in the US,\" Jose Zamora, regional director for the Americas, said. Having to reapply and wait for visa extensions every 240 days would have created an undue burden on journalists, he explained, especially now that the Trump administration is honing in on social media posts and news coverage that could be perceived as unaligned with its agenda. \"If you have to do it so often, and they have to do a review of everything you published... we have seen this pattern where the administration is trying to control the narrative and silence the journalists,\" Zamora said. The Foreign Press Association said it is \"pleased\" with the outcome, but urged caution among visa holders. \"We will study the findings carefully but still worry that bureaucratic practices on issuing visas and monitoring the work of foreign journalists might not reflect the spirit of the judgement,\" the body's president, Ian Williams, told MEE. \"It is clear that there is deep prejudice and malice motivating the Administration’s moves to restrict journalists' visas and we urge all media people to prepare carefully for traveling.\" Foreign journalists on an I-Visa, of which some 37,000 were admitted into the US in 2024, are permitted to work only at international news organisations headquartered overseas, DHS data shows. Reuters, BBC, Sky News, and Al Jazeera are just a few of the best-known news outlets whose staff would have been heavily impacted by the changes. Members of the foreign press are dispatched to the US as journalists who understand both their audiences back home and the machinations of US politics and culture, providing nuanced coverage and broadening the lens on their stories. It is for this reason they have been permitted to stay for several years - by both Republican and Democratic administrations - while they travel a vast country, learn its ways and complexities, and build trusted sources along the way. US Politics Washington News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
