    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Israeli military launches rare ground operation in Gaza beyond Yellow Line",
    summary: "Hamas condemns the incursion, which the Israeli military says is dismantling a \"terror tunnel\", as a \"flagrant\" ceasefire violation.",
    preview: "Israeli ground forces have launched a rare operation in part of the central Gaza Strip outside their control, after issuing evacuation orders for residents. Witnesses said tanks advanced east of Nuseirat refugee camp and rolled onto the territory's main north to south road under the cover of artillery strikes. The Israeli military said the \"targeted\" operation aimed to dismantle a \"terror tunnel\" west of the so-called Yellow Line, which marks the more than 60% of Gaza's territory occupied by Israel.",
    body: "Israeli ground forces have launched a rare operation in part of the central Gaza Strip outside their control, after issuing evacuation orders for residents.\n\nWitnesses said tanks advanced east of Nuseirat refugee camp and rolled onto the territory's main north to south road under the cover of artillery strikes.\n\nThe Israeli military said the \"targeted\" operation aimed to dismantle a \"terror tunnel\" west of the so-called Yellow Line, which marks the more than 60% of Gaza's territory occupied by Israel.\n\nThe Palestinian armed group Hamas, which controls the rest of Gaza, accused Israel of a \"flagrant violation\" of the October 2025 ceasefire agreement.\n\nViolence has continued in Gaza over the past 13 months, with near-daily Israeli strikes, and living conditions for the 2.1 million people there remain dire.\n\nEarly on Wednesday afternoon, Israeli military body Cogat's Arabic spokesperson announced the start of the incursion and warned Palestinians living in an area north of Nuseirat and Bureij refugee camp to stay away for their own safety.\n\nIt also said a section of the north to south Salah al-Din road that passed through the area would be closed to traffic.\n\nLater, the Israel Defense Forces (IDF) said it had begun a joint operation with the Shin Bet security service \"to dismantle a terror tunnel west of the Yellow Line that posed an immediate and direct threat to IDF troops\".\n\n\"IDF troops under the Southern Command are deployed in the area in accordance with the ceasefire agreement and will continue to operate to remove any immediate threat and thwart attempts by the Hamas terrorist organisation to harm Israeli civilians or IDF troops, or to violate the agreement,\" it added.\n\nHamas warned in a statement that the advance of Israeli military vehicles beyond the Yellow Line and the evacuation of residents constituted \"a dangerous development and a flagrant violation of the ceasefire agreement\".\n\nFootage published by Palestinian media outlets and on social media showed families fleeing areas near Nuseirat camp following the announcement.\n\nIn one video verified by BBC Verify, a man can be seen ducking after hearing artillery fire and then running along Salah al-Din road, about 1.5km (0.9 miles) west of the Yellow Line. \"The Israeli occupation now is firing randomly near the houses and tents of people,\" he says.\n\nAutomatic gunfire can be heard in another video filmed just to the south-east.\n\nA resident of Nuseirat who was forced to flee told BBC Arabic's Middle East Daily programme: \"The situation is now extremely dire, with Israeli forces firing indiscriminately at areas where displaced people have sought shelter.\"\n\n\"We couldn't take any of our belongings with us - not even our blankets, pillows, or clothes. We had no choice but to leave everything behind.\"\n\nA second resident also reported \"indiscriminate gunfire and shelling\" and said there had been \"numerous casualties, including fatalities\".\n\nAsked about reported fatalities, the IDF sent a general statement about the operation.\n\n- Israel moves Yellow Line deeper into Gaza, satellite images show\n\n- Published16 January\n\nGaza's Hamas-run Civil Defence agency said three people were killed and others were wounded in three separate Israeli drone strikes on Wednesday.\n\nOne man was killed in Nuseirat, the second to the north in Gaza City, and the third in the central town of Deir al-Balah, it added.\n\nThe Israeli military said the first two strikes targeted \"terrorists\" and that it was not aware of the third incident, according to AFP news agency.\n\nAt least 1,658 people have been killed in Israeli attacks in Gaza since the ceasefire began last year, according to the territory's Hamas-run health ministry, whose figures are considered reliable by the UN.\n\nThe Israeli military says six of its soldiers have been killed over the same period.\n\nMeanwhile, more than 1.9 million people - about 90% of Gaza's population - remain displaced, with 1.4 million of them living in tents or makeshift shelters, according to the UN.\n\nReconstruction has yet to begin in Gaza, where an estimated 82% of all structures have been destroyed or damaged during the war, as US President Donald Trump's peace plan has largely stalled.\n\nThe disarmament of Hamas has been a key sticking point.\n\nThe group has agreed to give up its weapons in exchange for a phased withdrawal of Israeli troops from the 60% of Gaza they currently occupy. But Israel has insisted that Hamas must fully disarm before any pullout.\n\nThe war was triggered by the Hamas-led attack on southern Israel on 7 October 2023, when about 1,200 people were killed and 251 others were taken hostage.\n\nSince then, more than 74,700 people have been killed in Gaza, the health ministry says.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published2 days ago\n\n- Published3 days ago\n\n- Published6 days ago",
