    title: "US urges respect of ceasefire in Western Sahara as UN peacekeepers leave",
    summary: "The UN peacekeeping mission to the territory, contested between Morocco and independence-seeking rebel, has not been renewed.",
    preview: "Morocco The US ambassador to the United Nations, Mike Waltz, on Tuesday called for calm and full respect of the ceasefire in the Western Sahara. Speaking in the Moroccan capital, Rabat, his comment come as the United Nations peacekeepers begin leaving the disputed territory. The mineral-rich Western Sahara is largely controlled by Morocco but for decades the Algeria-backed Polisario Front has pushed for independence. Washington backs Rabat’s claim. \"All parties must refrain from provocations and escalation, and we must remain committed to the ceasefire.",
    body: "Morocco\n\nThe US ambassador to the United Nations, Mike Waltz, on Tuesday called for calm and full respect of the ceasefire in the Western Sahara.\n\nSpeaking in the Moroccan capital, Rabat, his comment come as the United Nations peacekeepers begin leaving the disputed territory.\n\nThe mineral-rich Western Sahara is largely controlled by Morocco but for decades the Algeria-backed Polisario Front has pushed for independence.\n\nWashington backs Rabat’s claim.\n\n\"All parties must refrain from provocations and escalation, and we must remain committed to the ceasefire. And I know that will be the case here from the government, Morocco,\" said Waltz.\n\nThe UN peacekeeping mission in the territory, MINURSO, saw its mandate expire last month after the UN Security Council failed to adopt a resolution renewing it.\n\nMorocco has reiterated its commitment to a ceasefire, despite the Polisario declaring in 2020 that it would resume its armed struggle.\n\n\"We call on the Polisario to rejoin that ceasefire, to honour it in full, and to demonstrate a commitment to peace,\" Waltz said.\n\nWashington will also \"continue working with all parties and with the United Nations, as appropriate, towards a lasting settlement\", the envoy said.\n\nSeveral European nations, including the former colonial power Spain, have in recent years backed Rabat’s autonomy plan as a way to break decades of diplomatic deadlock over Western Sahara.\n\nMINURSO, was created in 1991 to organise a self-determination referendum which has never taken place.\n\nThe mission's chief, Russian diplomat Alexander Ivanko, said on Monday that the peacekeepers had officially begun withdrawing from the territory, but that the process might take months.\n\n\"We are trying to make January 31 the final deadline for our presence here,\" he told a Moroccan outlet.\n\nWashington had proposed extending the peacekeeping mission while effectively dropping the requirement for a referendum and making Morocco's autonomy plan the basis for any future talks.\n\nEleven of 15 council members supported that approach, but Russia rejected it as one-sided. Instead, Moscow sought to renew MINURSO unchanged, only for Washington to block the move.",
